/*! \page use Use guide

\addindex use
\tableofcontents

Running toffy means running one program, `toffyRunner`, over one XML config
file. Which filters run, in what order and on which data is all in the config;
the program itself has four options.

\section use_build Building

\code
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build -j
\endcode

Boost and OpenCV are required. PCL (the 3D and point cloud filters) and the
proprietary BTA SDK (Bluetechnix cameras) are optional and detected at
configure time; configure with `-DWITHOUT_PCL=ON` to build without PCL. The
library is `libtoffy.so` and the example application is
`build/apps/toffyRunner`.

\section use_run Running

\code
./build/apps/toffyRunner -c config.xml
\endcode

The complete option list, from `toffyRunner --help`:

<table>
<tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>-c [ --config ] arg</code></td><td><code>config.xml</code></td>
    <td>Path to the config file</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>-s [ --sleepDelay ] arg</code></td><td><code>30</code></td>
    <td>Milliseconds to wait between frames. <code>0</code> waits for a
        keypress in an image window</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>-f [ --output2File ] arg</code></td><td><code>false</code></td>
    <td>Write the program output to a file instead of the console</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--help</code></td><td></td><td>Print this and exit</td></tr>
</table>

`toffyRunner` serves nothing. It reads the config, builds the filter bank and
then runs one frame per iteration of its loop; the only thing it opens is the
OpenCV windows that the viewer filters ask for.

\section use_config The config file

The file below is the checked-in example at
`docs/configDocs/xmls/examples/config.xml`: a Bluetechnix camera as the source,
an amplitude threshold, and two image views of the depth and amplitude images.
Every element below `<toffy>` names a filter, and each one takes `name`,
`inputs`, `outputs` and `options` children — see \ref configuration.

\include examples/config.xml

\section use_stop Stopping

Press <b>q</b> in any OpenCV window. The loop reads a key after every frame and
ends the run when that key is `q`; any other key — and the `-1` that
`cv::waitKey()` returns when nothing was pressed — leaves it running. With
`-s 0` the wait is indefinite, so the pipeline advances one frame per keypress
instead of one per `sleepDelay` milliseconds.

Ctrl-C does **not** stop a run cleanly on Linux. `main.cpp` defines a SIGINT
handler that would end the loop, but the `sigaction()` call that would install
it is commented out, so Ctrl-C gets the default disposition and the process
dies where it stands. (The `SetConsoleCtrlHandler()` path that does work is
inside `#ifdef MSVC`.)

\section use_examples More configs

`apps/configs/` holds working configs for the cameras and for threaded and
merged filter banks (`thread_config.xml`, `merge_config22.xml`, `2d.xml`, the
`g*_config.xml` set), and `docs/configDocs/xmls/` holds the per-filter
reference configs that the \ref configuration page documents.

\section use_web Where did the web interface go?

Earlier versions of this page described a browser UI on `localhost:9999`, an
application called `minimal_toffy`, a `WITH_EXAMPLES` CMake flag and HTML
installed under `/opt/toffy/html`. None of those are in this repository. The
web control layer was dropped in `54d9577` and lives on in the separate
**toffy-oatpp** project; what it left behind — four installed headers, a
`WITH_CONTROL` hook and three dead command line options — was removed in
`cleanup/plan/controller-extraction.md`. If you are looking for that UI, look
there; if you are looking for the pipeline, you are already reading the right
page.

*/
